Sorts the reduced string of a repetitive block with bz2-ng's sais-lite
recursion (lib/sort_recursion.h) instead of libsais's own. Applies after
0002-bwt-row.patch.

After the top level has named the LMS substrings, libsais_main_8u sorts
the reduced string with libsais_main_32s_entry. When bzng_rec_repetitive
finds the block repetitive, it calls bzng_rec_sort_main instead, with the
same reduced string, suffix array and free space; both leave the suffix
array of the reduced string in SA[0..m) and touch nothing outside
SA[0..n + fs), so the top level's work after the call is the same. Both
functions are bz2-ng's, in lib/sort_libsais.c and lib/sort_recursion.h,
which sort_libsais.c includes before this file: with the patch applied
this file no longer compiles on its own.

bzng_rec_sort_main needs at a level of length n with alphabet k free
space of at least k, and every level has at least n - 1 >= k: libsais
gives this call at least m - 1 (docs/spec.md section 4.3), and a level
hands its own recursion either fs + n - 2m, which with m <= n/2 keeps
free space less length from falling, or, where it keeps its list of LMS
positions, at least the recursion's length. It allocates nothing.

bzng_rec_repetitive takes the block as repetitive when its reduced string
has at most one distinct name per 8 entries and more than 0.42 of its
first 8,192 entries are LMS positions of that string (the last entry of
that window counted as L-type). Measured over every
level-9 block of the MC:Z, XA:Z and quality corpora (16 KiB, 64 KiB and
whole-file streams) and of Silesia: every MC:Z block's reduced string has
LMS density 0.44-0.50 and every other block's 0.19-0.41.

Measured on the M4 with Apple clang 21, timing both recursions on each
block's own reduced string, back to back from the same input: the sais-
lite recursion takes 0.59-0.64 of libsais's time on MC:Z (every block
0.54-0.86) and 0.75-1.48 elsewhere (1.2-1.5 on XA:Z, quality and most of
Silesia whole files), which is why only repetitive blocks take it.

Measured end to end at level 9 against an identical-binary control, 5
rounds in rotating order, MC:Z 16 KiB / 64 KiB / whole: Graviton4 clang 18
1.03 / 1.03 / 1.04 and gcc 13 1.02 / 1.03 / 1.04; Granite Rapids gcc 13
1.06 / 1.05 / 1.03 and clang 18 1.04 / even / even; Zen 5 gcc 13 1.02 /
1.02 / 1.02 and clang 18 0.97 / 0.96 / 0.97; the M4 with Apple clang 21
1.17 / 1.21 / 1.26. Every XA:Z, quality-string and Silesia cell is even on
every machine. The loss under clang on Zen 5 is accepted: the two pairings
docs/spec.md section 3 ranks first both gain.

--- a/libsais.c
+++ b/libsais.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
  *
  * 0001-keep-lms-positions.patch
  * 0002-bwt-row.patch
+ * 0003-repetitive-recursion.patch
  */
 
 /*--
@@ -7000,7 +7001,15 @@
         sa_sint_t names = libsais_renumber_and_gather_lms_suffixes_omp(SA, n, m, fs, threads, thread_state);
         if (names < m)
         {
-            if (libsais_main_32s_entry(SA + n + fs - m, SA, m, names, fs + n - 2 * m, threads, thread_state) != 0)
+            /*
+             * Below a repetitive block, bz2-ng's sais-lite recursion
+             * (lib/sort_recursion.h, included before this file) in place
+             * of libsais's own, with the same arguments and result.
+             */
+            sa_sint_t rc = bzng_rec_repetitive(SA + n + fs - m, m, names)
+                ? bzng_rec_sort_main(SA + n + fs - m, SA, fs + n - 2 * m, m, names)
+                : libsais_main_32s_entry(SA + n + fs - m, SA, m, names, fs + n - 2 * m, threads, thread_state);
+            if (rc != 0)
             {
                 return -2;
             }
